VL  - 141
C7  - 104158
DO  - 10.1016/j.pce.2025.104158
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022200880?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Applied Sciences (Applied Geology), Curtin University, Sarawak, Miri, 98009, Malaysia
AB  - Pakistan's western margin represents one of the most tectonically active regions in South Asia, owing to the oblique convergence between the Indian and Eurasian plates. This study evaluates and focuses on fault mapping and neotectonic characterization of two key structural domains: Chaman Fault System (CFS) and Sulaiman Fold-Thrust Belt (SFTB). By integrating remote sensing, GIS-based geomorphic analysis, Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (InSAR) deformation data, seismic catalogs and published geodetic measurements this study delineates active fault strands and quantifies neotectonic activities. Results indicated significant strike-slip motion along CFS with slip rates ranging from 8.8 to 12 mm/yr and localized peaks up to 18 mm/yr. In SFTB the Karahi Fault exhibits right-lateral motion of up to 15 mm/yr while Harnai Fault appears largely aseismic. Hypsometric integrals and valley morphometric indices support differential uplift and block rotation which is consistent with “bookshelf” tectonic model. Seismicity patterns including major historical earthquakes align well with identified fault segments and deformation zones. This multidisciplinary approach underscores the importance of neotectonic studies for seismic hazard assessment and provides a framework for future tectonic monitoring and geodynamic modeling in Pakistan. © 2025 The Author.
