AD  - National Centre For Geodesy, Indian Institute Of Technology, Uttar Pradesh, Kanpur, India
AD  - Indian Institute Of Remote Sensing (ISRO), Dehradun, India
AB  - Differential Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (DInSAR) observations in this study reveal localized ground deformation associated with the 2019 Mw 5.6 Kashmir earthquake. The deformation is primarily concentrated between the NE-SW trending Samwal Fault and NW-SE trending Jhelum Fault, consistent with the region's known tectonic features. DInSAR-derived phase profiles show uplift of approximately 0.27 meters south of the Samwal Fault and subsidence of around 0.16 meters to the north in the line-of-sight direction, indicating fault-controlled deformation. Despite its moderate magnitude, the earthquake caused considerable infrastructure damage, drawing attention to the influence of local geological conditions on seismic impacts. Contributing factors include sedimentary basin effects, fault geometry, and ongoing crustal stress accumulation. The region's complex tectonic framework, shaped by the interaction of major fault systems, underpins its high seismic potential. These findings highlight the effectiveness of DInSAR in delineating subtle ground motion and identifying active fault segments. They underscore the importance of incorporating detailed geological and remote sensing data into seismic hazard assessments. The study not only enhances understanding of the 2019 earthquake's mechanics but also informs future risk mitigation efforts across the vulnerable Himalayan foothills. © 2025 IEEE.
