AB  - Numcrous significant carlhquakes have occurred in the Tibetan Plateau since the recent decades years, attracting considcrablc attention front scholars both domestieally and intcrnationally. These major earthquakcs often oeeur along enormous fault zoncs, which not only exhibit intense activity in the late Quaternary period but also possess structural conditions necessary for the development and oecurrence of strong earthquakcs. The Karakorum fault Stands out as a large-seale dextral strike-slip fault in the western part of the Tibetan Plateau. The part south of the Lungmu Co fault more pronounced activity and has experienced the Pulan Ms7. 0 earthquakc in 1883. Understanding the most recent deformation behavior of this fault and analyzing its seismogenic characteristics are crucial in identifying the segmented activity patterns and surface rupture features of the fault zonc. Bascd on high resolution remote sensing imagc Interpretation and field geological survey, the late Quaternary segmented activity of the southern section of the Karakorum fault and the Chaxikang surface rupture zonc have been mcticulously investigated. The findings reveal that the southern section of the fault zonc can be further dividcd into thrce primary Segments: Ladakh-Chaxikang, Kunsha-Bar and Menshi-Gongzhu Co segment. These Segments are characterized by dextral strike-slip, dextral strike-slip normal faulting and dextral strike-slip with a horsetail normal fault, respectively. Mcasurements taken from the surface rupture zonc indicate a minimum horizontal displacement of 8~9 m. Considering the approximately 200 km length of the surface rupture, it is estimated that the magnitude of the earthquake could be ~7. 5. The Karakorum fault zone, which controls the matcrial extrusion in the western plateau, gradually transforms from a rigid block migration mode in the northwest section to a morc dispersed arc-shaped normal fault and strike slip deformation in the southeast section. © 2025 Geological Society of China. All rights reserved.
