AD  - Solid Waste Management Expert, Mosaic Advanced Solutions LLP, Swachh Bharat Mission (SBM), Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA), New Delhi, India
AB  - Landslides are among the most damaging natural hazards in mountainous terrains in short order. This acts differently in various parts of the country due to climatic conditions, geotectonic, and other anthropogenic pressures, especially in north-eastern India and the Western Ghats. Prolong landslides had disastrous consequences, causing enormous economic losses, land degradation, and affecting the social fabric that was most recorded during monsoons and cyclone periods. The present objective focuses on delineating the vulnerable zones in eastern Madikeri taluk in Western Ghats through thematic mapping in the GIS platform. The study area is a part of seismic zone III, representing ample neotectonic signatures, and is very close to the geologically active fault scarp. Earlier earthquakes in the study area had recorded 3.4 on the Richter scale and influenced the hydrological cavities in the sloppy regions. Many mild tremors were recorded in the recent past that traveled about 10-15km or more. Required important thematic layers were efficiently digitized by Survey of India (SoI) toposheets of 1:50,000 scale, Landsat images, and digital elevation model (DEM) map through GIS insights. Lithology, geomorphology, drainage, lineament, rainfall, soil, slope, land use land cover, and other related features were overlaid one above the other and the vulnerable zones using the AHP method. Geospatial technologies contribute to outlining these zones for better mitigation and preparedness plans in minimizing the loss of life, land resources, and property for future land sustainability. The final results portray vulnerable and safer zones for future rescue and human safety, which may be utilized for similar geological terrains. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved..
