AD  - College of Earth and Planetary Sciences, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100049, China
AB  - Cobalt and nickel are critical Strategie metals. However, China lacks Co-Ni resources, and the deposits that are ultra-enriehed in both Co and Ni. Five-element (Ni-Co-As-Ag-Bi) vein deposit is a rare type of hydrothermal deposit charaeterized primarily by arsenides. These deposits can attain exceptionally high grades of Co and Ni (exceeding 10%), and are also associated with valuable byproduets such as Ag, Au and U, thus possessing signifieant economic value. These deposits are rare globally, predominantly found in North America and Europe, with only two in China. The ore minerals primarily consist of arsenides and sulfarsenides, together with minor sulfides and native elements with fern-like, spherieal and dendritic textures. They have a sequence from early to late stages, ineluding oxide—»-native element (simple substance)—»-arsenide—»-sulfarsenide—»-sulfide—»-sulfosalt stage with an elemental general formation order of Ni —> Co —? Fe and As —? S. We summarized the spatiotemporal distribution patterns and enriehment mechanisms of five-element vein deposits, from which a four-metallogenic-factors model is proposed as follows; (1) mafic-uitramafie rocks are usually eonsidered the souree of Ni and Co; (2) intermediate to aeidic intrusions provide heat or fluids for leaching and migrating ore-forming materials; (3) additionally, intense geological struetures provide transport Channels for fluids and Space for mineral preeipitation; (4) reducing strata contributes reduetants (e. g., CH4 and Fe), which react with ore-bearing fluids in strong reducing aetion, causing mineral preeipitation. Furthermore, this paper proposed some exploration methods for five-element vein deposits: (1) remote sensing imagery can be processed to identify various plutons; (2) stratigraphic data on organic carbon content can be reviewed to identify high-earbon strata; and (3) aeromagnetic data combined with fault density maps can be used to locate active fault zones. By integrating these datasets, we can identify prospective exploration areas. Furthermore, fieldwork focusing on indicator minerals like annabergite and erythrite can improve the exploration efficieney. © 2025 Science Press. All rights reserved.
